00:01:30.878 --> 00:01:33.510
OK, that's not bad.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.050
Good.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.060
So yeah, as I said,
questions at the very end.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.630
Now what we'll be doing today.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:44.790
Today we'll do an overview
of four main compute options

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.550
in Google Cloud, including
Compute Engine, Container

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.922
Engine, App Engine,
and Cloud Functions.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.860
The purpose of each
section is to give you

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.330
the sense of what
each service is doing

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:01.350
and when you might want to use
it for your existing workload

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.850
or for the workload
that you want

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:07.410
to consider to bring into
Google Cloud at some point.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.551
Also we'll do the
customer case from Tentoo.

00:02:10.551 --> 00:02:12.110
Freek will present it.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.700
And at the end,
we will do a recap

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.800
so to see whether we answered,
or not, the questions

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.170
on where should I
actually run my code.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:28.170
So there was a
picture about my goal.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.790
Come on, come back.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.230
You can do it.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:36.540
So again, the goal is to
remove any ambiguity that

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.460
might be existing
up until this point,

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.390
and show you where to run what
and where to migrate what.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.840
And also, when you might want
to choose certain trade offs

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.000
of one service versus another.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.570
Without further ado, Compute
Engine, or virtual machines,

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.220
or infrastructure as a service
within the Google Cloud.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.810
Obviously, first of all,
that's virtual machines,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.240
block storage, and networking.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.520
Like in typical infrastructure,
the service framework,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.720
you can request the
virtual machine's YUI.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.900
You can request virtual
machine's YCli clients

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:08.760
via the API.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:13.990
So all the usual goodness which
is typical for public clouds.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:18.090
Now a few specific traits, or
characteristics, I would say,

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.920
for, specifically,
Compute Engine.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.960
One is the virtual
machines start very fast.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.980
So average start time
is about 35 seconds.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.440
Another one is the
flexibility of configuration.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.190
So within Google Cloud,
you have the selection

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.430
of both a set of
pre-built configurations

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.850
for virtual machines, including
also high memory, high CPU

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.620
ones, and the custom
machine types.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.330
So you can assign
any virtual machine

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:46.650
to any ratio between
the vCPU and RAM

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.200
as much as your
configuration requires.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.080
What was recently introduced,
actually, like months ago,

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.350
is the configurations
with extended memory,

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:59.580
where now you don't have
the ratio of 6.5 gigabytes

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.610
RAM per vCPU,
meaning that, again,

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:06.760
if you need much RAM for
your CPU, you can do that.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.470
Finally, the choice
of vCPU platform.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.220
So across all the regions
of the Google Cloud,

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:17.279
we're on about five generations
of Intel processors,

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:20.910
and Skylake was the one
most recently introduced.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.620
So one of the features
in Google Cloud

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.720
right now allows
you to specify when

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.856
you create the
virtual machine, which

00:04:27.856 --> 00:04:29.730
is the minimum generation
of Intel processors

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.020
which you would like to have.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.810
And finally, this
is my favorite one,

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:37.530
is the self-service
migration at no extra cost.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.990
So if right now, there is some
configuration in another cloud

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.460
or on a on-premise
virtualization environment

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.800
that you are considering
to move to Google Cloud

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.560
and test how it works there,
we have the built-in migration

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.830
tool link into Google
Cloud at no extra cost,

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:57.440
and which can migrate VMs
from pretty much anywhere.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.200
Now when would Compute
Engine be useful?

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.310
So first and obvious
choice for the good fit

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.240
is lift and shift
into Google Cloud.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.010
And by lift and
shift, I'm not only

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.590
meaning take some
legacy workload

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:12.860
and try to move it in the Cloud
and get very disappointed.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.050
I actually mean
any virtual machine

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:16.820
based configuration
that runs anywhere

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:20.290
on other public clouds, on
house hostings, on anything.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:22.710
So anything that runs
with full operating system

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.070
stack on someone's hypervisor
is a good candidate

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:27.950
to be put into Compute Engine.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:31.130
Another example is enterprise
workloads, maybe also requiring

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.130
licenses, so things
that might be running

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.680
on-premises hypervisor,
or things that might

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.390
be licensed on per VM basis.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:44.120
On per core basis, again, good
candidate for Compute Engine.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.850
Operating system kernel and
performance requirements.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:48.590
So there are many
companies-- there

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.630
are many pieces of
software that are developed

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:53.300
with a specific stack in
mind, with a specific set

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.960
of dependencies, with a specific
choice of operating system,

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.130
it might be FreeBSD, actually.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:05:59.570
In such case,
obviously, you would

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:01.550
want to control the whole--

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.050
you would want to control the
whole compute host that you're

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.380
running it into, and
Compute Engine would easily

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.060
allow you to do that.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.420
And finally, network
protocols beyond HTTPS.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:13.670
So if the application
of a service that you're

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:16.120
putting into Cloud needs
to talk to something

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.190
that is not just plain HTTPS
accessible over browser

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:23.424
or kernel, in many cases, the
load balancers and the services

00:06:23.424 --> 00:06:25.340
that you will need for
this will be available,

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.750
mainly on Compute Engine.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.750
Now what would be
the constraints

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.060
if you choose to go
with Compute Engine?

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.990
First of all, there is a
certain scale and speed floor.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:37.190
And it's not like
it's very high,

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:38.900
so 30 seconds per
virtual machine.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.426
But again, if you're going
for a web-scale workload

00:06:42.426 --> 00:06:44.300
and you need to be able
to scale up and scale

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.610
down very dynamically, it
might become a constraint.

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:48.020
And this might be
the point where

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.769
you would start thinking
about containers,

00:06:49.769 --> 00:06:51.690
if you are not doing so yet.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.730
So still good to keep in mind.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:57.330
Another point is self-managing
configurations and updates.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:01.550
So when you run on virtual
machines on Compute Engine,

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:04.880
Google can afford certain
complexity of management

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:08.180
using managed instance groups,
when Google Cloud will take

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:11.930
care of updating your VMs from
time to time using the updated

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:12.814
VM images.

00:07:12.814 --> 00:07:14.480
But still, the degree
of things that you

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.130
will have to manage
within the VM

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:20.570
is kind of higher, compared to
even with Docker containers.

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:22.700
And finally, my
favorite argument

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.700
is am I making the
most of my VMs?

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:27.890
Now this has been quite
typical for the last,

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:30.690
I would say, seven years, as
containerization and Docker

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:31.862
were growing.

00:07:31.862 --> 00:07:33.570
Because what was
happening is that people

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.090
were paying quite
significant money

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:38.150
for getting virtual machines,
or even bare metal hosts,

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:40.272
and keeping them underutilized.

00:07:40.272 --> 00:07:41.730
This is still
possible when you are

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.970
running on Compute Engine or
any other infrastructures,

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.960
or as platform, and making
not exactly optimal choice

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:52.130
when you buy VMs and
keep them underutilized.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:53.690
Luckily, within
Google Cloud, there

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.820
is a tool called rightsizing
recommendations, which will

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:57.069
indicate these things for you.

00:07:57.069 --> 00:07:58.910
So if you deployed
a bunch of VMs,

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:01.155
and they are stalling
most of the time,

00:08:01.155 --> 00:08:05.510
Google Cloud will recommend
you to at least downsize them.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:07.690
OK, quick demo on
Compute Engine.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:09.230
Can we please switch
on the laptop?

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:18.070
This is just a very basic
one, just to give you

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.930
the sense of how it looks.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:22.150
I will zoom in a little bit.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:29.520
So that's Compute Engine,
here on Google Cloud console.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:31.270
All the products and
services are actually

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:33.396
called through the
menu on the left.

00:08:33.396 --> 00:08:35.020
I'm getting the
Compute Engine, and I'm

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.539
going to create a
new virtual machine.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.730
So I hit Create Instance.

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:42.010
I decide how I will name it.

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:45.420
Well, let's say it
will be hello next.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.954
I decide where to place it.

00:08:46.954 --> 00:08:48.370
So the list that
you see right now

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:49.840
is the list of
availability zones

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.000
currently available
at Google Cloud.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.870
So I will do Europe West 1d.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:56.620
That's Belgium.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:58.450
Now the configuration.

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:03.190
So I can choose between the
list of predefined types,

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:06.740
like I told you earlier,
with high memory or high CPU.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:08.510
Or I can hit
Customize, and that's

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:09.950
where all the goodness starts.

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:13.290
So just with easily
dragging and dropping,

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:16.040
I can increase the size of
VM, decrease the size of VM.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.410
And here on the right,
I will be also seeing

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:22.280
how the price estimate
for changes on the way.

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:24.680
The extended memory feature,
which I mentioned earlier.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:28.190
So if I don't like the
default ratio on memory,

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:31.310
I can do something like
this, 360 gigabytes of RAM,

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:32.750
18 with CPUs.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:36.690
I wonder what sort of things
I would be doing there.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:37.280
Yeah.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.360
And finally, the CPU platform.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.340
So as you see, here
in the Europe West 1d,

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:46.790
I have the choice between
Intel Haskell or later,

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:48.630
or Intel Skylake or later.

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:52.160
So let's go with Intel
Skylake, the latest stuff.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.760
And I will get back to the
standard configurations, just

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.620
one vCPU.

00:09:57.620 --> 00:09:59.810
Then, obviously, I will
choose the boot disk.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:02.490
So which version system
do I want to start there.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:06.320
So typical Linux flavors
are available here.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.850
Also there is Windows somewhere
here, yes, 2016, 2012.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:13.460
And also, you can actually bring
in your own images-- that's

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.740
also important to remember--
from existing cloud

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:18.620
or from a custom built pipeline.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:21.090
So it's not just limited to
what Google offered here.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:24.020
A few quick checkboxes to
enable some default firewall

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.750
rules, like HTTP, HTTPS.

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:31.325
And further on, there
is a menu for doing

00:10:31.325 --> 00:10:33.450
a bunch of other things,
like run in startups tips,

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.660
for example, to instantiate
the initial configuration

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:37.925
of the virtual machine.

00:10:37.925 --> 00:10:40.500
We will not be
doing anything here.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.160
Let's go and hit Create.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:45.320
So while it's creating, I
will mention one more thing,

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:47.610
is persistent disks.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:51.386
So each virtual machine,
you can attach up to--

00:10:51.386 --> 00:10:53.010
actually, I don't
remember the number--

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.320
you can attach multiple
persistent disks to it,

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.240
and they will be network backed.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:59.280
So they will be placed
within Google network,

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.950
attached over network,
replicated, encrypted.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:03.820
Very secure, very
high performance.

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:06.570
Obviously, the bigger disk you
make, the higher throughput

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.680
you will get, and
if you will opt

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:12.105
for SSD as well, because that's
the distributed file systems.

00:11:12.105 --> 00:11:14.020
That's just how they work.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:15.360
So let's get back to the demo.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:18.240
I have my hello next here.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:23.310
What I can do now is I can just
hit SSH and get to the terminal

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:24.450
of my virtual machine.

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.895
So that's the part which
many folks, especially

00:11:26.895 --> 00:11:28.770
from development community,
are appreciating,

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:33.180
because Google Cloud UI is built
in a way when you can pretty

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.300
much skip any configurations
on your own machine

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:39.720
in order to be able to get
to the VMs and the resources

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:41.080
that you're doing here.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:47.970
So both a web shell
for SSH, and also what

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.510
is called Cloud Console.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:53.580
OK, so that was a quick
demo on the Compute Engine.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:56.930
Can we please switch
back to the slides?

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:57.930
Nice.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:01.500
So now we've got into
Container Engine,

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:04.590
or managed Kubernetes, or
container as a service.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.300
Quick raise of hands, please.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:09.145
Please raise your hand if
you are working with Docker.

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:14.340
OK, half a room.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:15.910
And will you please
raise your hands

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.880
if you are working with Docker
using some orchestration tools,

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:22.100
like Kubernetes
or Docker's forum.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:24.530
OK, that's also very good.

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:30.880
So a quick recap on containers
for those who might not

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:33.056
be familiar with Docker.

00:12:33.056 --> 00:12:34.930
Containerization and
Docker Containerization,

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:38.230
namely, allows us to virtualize
the workloads on operating

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:41.590
system level, effectively
encapsulating the application

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:44.650
code and these dependencies
into what is called Container,

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:47.350
and at the same time,
allowing different isolated

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:50.980
workloads to share the same
operating system kernel.

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:52.970
What this gives us
is a lot of benefits,

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:53.936
including portability.

00:12:53.936 --> 00:12:55.810
So you can run containers
in different clouds

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.840
and different Docker hosts.

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.900
Also the performance
and the density.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:05.360
You remember the argument
about am I getting the most out

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:06.100
of my VMs?

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.680
When you run with Docker, you
can pack much more workloads

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:11.080
in the same VM and not
care about dependencies

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.530
and overlapping wisdom.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:15.640
And Docker itself
is not hard to use,

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:18.070
especially when you
start practicing

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:19.415
using docs on one machine.

00:13:19.415 --> 00:13:20.540
It's quite straightforward.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:24.030
It's not much harder
than installing packages

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:26.194
on top of a Linux box.

00:13:26.194 --> 00:13:27.610
Where the complexity
comes is when

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:30.790
you have 50 plus Docker hosts,
50 just a random number.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:34.090
That's what I consider to
be uncomfortable for me.

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:37.570
So you'll have to poke every
Docker host individually

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:39.890
and tell them run this
container, expose this port,

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:40.870
connect to this port.

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:43.780
And the whole complexity comes
in when the application becomes

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:48.080
truly distributed and involved
with multiple containers.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:51.580
So what helps here is the
project called Kubernetes.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:54.400
So that's an
orchestration platform

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.110
for containerized workloads.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:58.630
Initially inspired
by Google, right now

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:04.120
developed by many companies,
including Google, Red Hat,

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:05.710
and others.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:07.570
What it allows,
in general, is it

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:10.540
allows the declarative
definition of application.

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:12.440
So we don't care
anymore about servers.

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:15.280
We don't care anymore about
individual containers.

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:17.770
You just describe which
components your application

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:20.590
consists of, how they should
connect to each other, how they

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:22.180
should discover each other.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:24.220
And Kubernetes will
take care of where

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:28.030
to put these components
within your cluster,

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:30.370
on top of Docker containers,
and also how many

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:31.330
replicas to create.

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:33.130
So how many examples
of my web server

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:36.029
do I need, how many examples
of my database do I need,

00:14:36.029 --> 00:14:37.820
and a bunch of other
things, like scale out

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:40.484
or scale in and load balancing.

00:14:40.484 --> 00:14:42.150
Another important
point about Kubernetes

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:46.390
is that it's multi-cloud
and is platform agnostic.

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:49.030
So it runs on any
major cloud provider

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:51.950
and also on bare metal.

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.950
So what is Container Engine?

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:56.140
It's the managed
Kubernetes offering

00:14:56.140 --> 00:14:57.520
on top of Google Cloud.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.050
It automates management,
upgrades, deployment,

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:02.620
scaling of the cluster
around stable version,

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:06.400
and allows you also to use
so-called alpha clusters.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.470
So it pretty much offsets
all the complexity

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:10.840
of managing the cluster
itself, and lets

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:14.758
you focus on the application
and on deploying it.

00:15:14.758 --> 00:15:17.830
Along with that, it offers other
benefits of GCP infrastructure,

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:21.610
so global load balancing, very
powerful, multi-zone clusters.

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:26.230
So Kubernetes clusters,
which will span multiple,

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:29.397
will be availability zones
and be highly available.

00:15:29.397 --> 00:15:31.480
And also container
registering, container builder.

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:33.771
These are the things that
you will need anyway when you

00:15:33.771 --> 00:15:36.280
will be adopting containers.

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:38.016
Now when to use
Container Engine.

00:15:38.016 --> 00:15:40.390
So the great fit is obviously
containerized applications,

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:42.230
right of this.

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:44.840
What I would like to say is
that usually teams who want

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.750
Kubernetes, they
ask their bosses.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:47.650
We want Kubernetes.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:49.540
We want to run in this
particular platform,

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:51.880
or we want to run on
Dockers 4 or more NSM.

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:54.160
So if the team has
already adopted Docker,

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:58.540
they will probably, at certain
scale, need using Kubernetes.

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:01.420
Another example is Micro
Services based architectures.

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:05.320
So again, when one big monolith
of application developed

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:07.810
somewhere into
thousands becomes split

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:13.055
into a lot of small
services, often the amount

00:16:13.055 --> 00:16:15.430
of containers that you will
have to run becomes very big,

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:18.070
and you will need an
orchestration platform.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:19.240
Multi-hybrid Cloud.

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:20.750
As I already
mentioned, Kubernetes

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:21.980
runs on multiple platforms.

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:24.350
And it actually works.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:27.374
And again, network
protocols beyond HTTPS.

00:16:27.374 --> 00:16:29.040
Next section will be
App Engine, and you

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:34.470
will understand why I'm putting
so much emphasis on it so far.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:36.310
Best fit or constraints.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:38.085
You can run only
containerized workloads.

00:16:38.085 --> 00:16:40.210
So you cannot run virtual
machines with Kubernetes.

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:43.020
Actually, technically,
you can, but it's

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:46.000
outside of scope of this talk.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:47.600
Services oriented architecture.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:50.140
That's kind of the opposite
side of Micro Services based

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:50.860
architectures.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:55.060
So it's not like
you will want to,

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:58.990
you will have to
refactor the application

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:00.490
and split it into
services in order

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:01.990
to be truly efficient
in Kubernetes.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.210
You will not want to put
like old monolith, which

00:17:04.210 --> 00:17:06.700
doesn't scale horizontally
on Kubernetes,

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:09.069
and just get more
complexity for yourself.

00:17:09.069 --> 00:17:12.130
So the application
has to conform also.

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:14.440
And finally, imminent
CI/CD pipeline,

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:16.339
Continuous Integration,
Continuous Delivery.

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:18.297
If you will start producing
your own containers

00:17:18.297 --> 00:17:22.394
and deploying them at scale,
you will need your own practices

00:17:22.394 --> 00:17:26.670
of staged deployment,
development, automatic testing,

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:27.490
things like this.

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:34.270
So Kubernetes is-- it's sort
of a prerequisite for it.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:38.520
And one piece before we get into
Customer Case is App Engine.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:41.820
So App Engine is what we call
an original Google Cloud.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:46.482
It started back in 2008, before
we had the rest of the things.

00:17:46.482 --> 00:17:48.690
And it's a platform as a
service offering for Google,

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:56.830
which follows the mentality of
code first UX for developers.

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:58.980
So with App Engine,
we used to want,

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:01.110
and we still want,
and we still do it,

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:05.210
let developers focus purely
on code, deploying the code,

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:08.580
and no additional infrastructure
management required around

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:09.810
whatsoever.

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:12.330
The trade off for it is that
App Engine has, obviously,

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:14.070
certain ways of how
the applications have

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:17.040
to be written to be
able to run on it,

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:18.970
and sometimes it
enables some things,

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:21.330
sometimes it may put
additional constraints.

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:24.090
But the code first UX gets
you to the first deployment

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:26.700
in no time, with
no learning curve.

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:29.100
So you have fully managed,
no ops infrastructure,

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.070
load balancing, scale, and
everything is taken care of.

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:34.410
And what's important is
the lowest entry barrier

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:36.040
and shortest learning curve.

00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:38.160
So Kubernetes is
friendly to developers,

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:41.490
and it gets even more friendly
to developers with time.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:44.622
But still, like in my experience
and experience of many people,

00:18:44.622 --> 00:18:46.080
when you start
learning Kubernetes,

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:48.720
you have to get into this
whole world of Docker

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:52.080
containers, service
discovery, service management,

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:54.930
a lot of load balancing,
its own object

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:57.879
model, which is quite nice and
gets you additional experience.

00:18:57.879 --> 00:18:59.670
But if you want to
deploy something faster,

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:02.940
or if you're not interested
in infrastructure at all,

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:06.520
the barrier of entry on
App Engine is much lower.

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:10.660
So App Engine has
multiple runtime options

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:13.650
across two tiers that it
has, Standard and Flex.

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:17.700
What runtime means is what
sort of language environment

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.310
can App Engine support for you.

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:22.740
So today, it's multiple
versions of Python,

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.620
multiple versions of Java,
multiple versions of Go.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:29.780
And most importantly, it's .NET,
and also customer runtimes.

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.340
Customer runtimes have
been introduced recently

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:33.810
with App Engine Flex.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:35.790
And what happens is
that App Engine Flex

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:40.120
runs your workloads, not
like standard one, which

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:42.270
uses underlying
Google's infrastructure

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.280
work to run your workloads.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:47.560
But it runs Docker containers
within the virtual machines.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:51.360
So you see the similarity with
Kubernetes to some extent.

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:54.630
And this also enables
customized runtimes.

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:57.120
So if some language
is not supported

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:58.890
by App Engine at
the moment, this

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:01.420
can be added by you, by
Google, by certain parties.

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:03.870
That's possible.

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:06.300
The coolest, personally,
for me, thing for App Engine

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:07.864
is how the traffic
splitting works.

00:20:07.864 --> 00:20:09.780
That's something that I
will show in the demo.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:12.922
So all the load balancers
available in Google,

00:20:12.922 --> 00:20:14.880
in my opinion, are very
cool and very powerful.

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:17.380
But this one, it
just works magic.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:20.100
So doing A/B test and can shift
in traffic between the versions

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:23.580
is-- literally happens
on the wave of your hand.

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:26.300
And also App Engine also
supports Micro Services

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:26.930
architectures.

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:29.780
So we just talked about
it in Kubernetes section,

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:32.640
that the application is getting
split into smaller parts,

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:34.200
and services talk to each other.

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:41.250
You can also do it in App Engine
in a slightly less flexible

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:44.420
way, because App Engine has
certain restrictions that it

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:47.110
puts on terms of how the
code has to be structured,

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:48.712
how many applications
you can have,

00:20:48.712 --> 00:20:50.920
how many services you can
have within an application.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:52.003
But still, it is possible.

00:20:52.003 --> 00:20:54.210
So you don't have to run
monoliths on App Engine.

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:55.880
But if you're running--

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:57.820
if you plan to
develop something new,

00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:00.690
If you want to use your PC
and not just HTTP invocation,

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:05.230
probably you will steer
towards Kubernetes.

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:07.170
OK, so when to use App Engine.

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:08.850
Good fit.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:12.240
Applications that only need
HTTP as request and response.

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:15.150
That's the pattern,
together with stateless

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:16.120
serving applications.

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:17.580
That's a pattern for App Engine.

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:19.530
That's the web face
in applications

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:21.730
that usually don't
need to talk anywhere

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:25.390
but northbound to the
users that come in.

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:26.560
Mobile and web backends.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.340
So many of our
signature customers

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:31.310
running at very
large scale have been

00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:37.710
using App Engine to scale
horizontally very fast, very

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:41.160
reliably, and speed up the
development with this, again,

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:45.930
because the runtimes that we
offer are sufficient for it.

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:47.470
Rapidly scaling to high traffic.

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:49.590
What I just mentioned,
especially on App Engine

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:51.507
standard, which runs
on underlying Google

00:21:51.507 --> 00:21:53.090
infrastructure, as
I mentioned to you.

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:55.590
The time of scaling
is very fast.

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:58.590
So new instances can be added
almost instantly, even faster

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:01.050
than containers.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:02.470
What are the constraints?

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:06.180
So let's say I have a web app
or a mobile backend, whatever.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:08.820
What will I have to
deal with on App Engine?

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:11.550
First of all, if I choose
App Engine Standard, which

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:15.390
scales faster, costs less,
but has more restrictions,

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:17.400
I will have a set of
predefined runtimes,

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:19.920
and I will not have an
option of custom runtimes.

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:22.320
So if my runtime is not
available for App Engine

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:24.060
Standard, I will not
be able to use it.

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:26.901
Or I will have to
change my runtime.

00:22:26.901 --> 00:22:29.230
Can't you [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:32.440
Quite obvious, the same way
how restricted network and file

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:33.305
system access works.

00:22:33.305 --> 00:22:34.930
So when you run on
App Engine Standard,

00:22:34.930 --> 00:22:37.630
you cannot write into
your file system.

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:40.150
And the only network
connectivity that you can do

00:22:40.150 --> 00:22:41.770
is an out bound to the internet.

00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:44.420
You cannot talk within Google
Cloud, only with the APIs.

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:45.910
You cannot talk with--

00:22:45.910 --> 00:22:49.900
well, it's pretty much
like web face and hosting.

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:54.255
On Flex, it's a
bit more relaxed.

00:22:54.255 --> 00:22:56.380
So first of all, obviously,
we have standard Docker

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:57.070
constraints.

00:22:57.070 --> 00:23:00.140
So if it cannot be
put into Docker image,

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:02.890
it will not run on
App Engine Flex.

00:23:02.890 --> 00:23:05.350
And second, it's not scale to 0.

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:08.260
So App Engine Standard
can do the scale to 0, 3.

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:10.840
When there is no traffic,
it will scale to 1,

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:12.970
or even 0, instances.

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:13.956
Flex cannot do it.

00:23:13.956 --> 00:23:15.330
So Flex runs on
virtual machines,

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:18.405
so you will always have to
maintain some sort of capacity.

00:23:18.405 --> 00:23:19.780
So if you're
developing something

00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:22.900
that you want to keep on the
free tier and on a low traffic,

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:26.060
probably App Engine
Standard is a better choice.

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:28.460
And again, as I
mentioned earlier,

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:30.350
if you're starting
with a new application,

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:34.240
if you have an environment where
dev and ops are working very

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:37.210
closely-- and we'll talk
about it a bit later--

00:23:37.210 --> 00:23:40.030
if you don't yet
know how much it

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:42.700
will scale in terms of
amounts of components

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:47.670
and the size of the team, when
choosing between App Engine

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:50.920
and Kubernetes, make
sure that the structure

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:53.860
of projects in each
of the platforms

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:55.695
will allow you to work with it.

00:23:55.695 --> 00:23:57.070
Kubernetes will
be very flexible.

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:58.880
It can use multiple
container registries.

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:01.420
You can use multiple namespace
as multiple Google Cloud

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:02.250
projects.

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:03.710
App Engine is more prescriptive.

00:24:03.710 --> 00:24:05.710
So you have one
application per project,

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:07.630
multiple services
within the application,

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:09.580
and multiple versions
of the services.

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:12.580
For someone who just wants to
do the code first and knows

00:24:12.580 --> 00:24:14.980
the scope of the
development for whatever--

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:17.520
next half a year or 12 months--

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:18.490
it's enabling.

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:19.610
It makes you work faster.

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:21.443
It makes you care less
about things that you

00:24:21.443 --> 00:24:22.980
don't want to care about.

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:25.580
If the level of
uncertainty is higher,

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:27.250
it might happen
that it makes sense

00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:32.500
to invest in getting
up with Kubernetes.

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:35.330
OK, so quick demo on App Engine.

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:38.104
Can I please switch
to the laptop?

00:24:38.104 --> 00:24:39.520
This will be
actually faster even.

00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:51.570
OK, so here I have my
App Engine dashboard--

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:54.900
and it failed to load
because I don't have Wi-Fi.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:57.880
Interesting.

00:24:57.880 --> 00:24:58.710
Let's try again.

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:03.340
Oh, I'm not supposed
to have the Wi-Fi.

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:06.450
I am on the wired connection.

00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:07.950
Anyways.

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:12.456
So I have the--

00:25:12.456 --> 00:25:14.580
just a second, I will still
check the connectivity,

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:18.660
because it seems like I'm
having a bit of trouble here.

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:32.030
OK, so I lose the
wired connection.

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:34.440
I will try to do it
through the Wi-Fi.

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:35.300
I was still wired.

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:42.770
That's not something
that you would expect.

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:45.620
OK, so I have my App
Engine deployment.

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:50.832
And I have two versions
of my application running.

00:25:50.832 --> 00:25:52.040
Let's wait for it to show up.

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:57.100
No, no chance.

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:07.280
Yeah, we will not get there.

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:09.780
OK, we'll skip the App Engine
demo because of the networking

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:10.836
part.

00:26:10.836 --> 00:26:12.210
Please switch back
to the slides.

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:17.930
Sorry about that.

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:21.470
So before we hand over
to the customer example,

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:24.680
I will tell you the story
of all their App Engine now.

00:26:24.680 --> 00:26:26.840
So over there, I
have an application

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:29.720
running in two versions,
which each of them

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:32.450
displays a different version of
the website, a very simple one

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:33.770
like Hello, Next Amsterdam.

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:37.870
So I had to relay
this message to them.

00:26:37.870 --> 00:26:40.190
You remember, I
mentioned a load balancer

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:41.330
was a traffic splitter.

00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:44.570
So when you run with App Engine,
and you deploy a new version

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:47.450
of a website, or of
application, or whatever--

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:49.280
your ecommerce store--

00:26:49.280 --> 00:26:55.850
it's very easy to shift
the traffic in natural way,

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:59.930
like what is the percentage
of the users which

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:01.436
I want to go to
version 1, what is

00:27:01.436 --> 00:27:02.810
the percentage of
the users which

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:04.280
I want to go to version 2.

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:05.900
So this both enables
you to easily

00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:08.990
deploy, and your actions roll
back to them, and test them,

00:27:08.990 --> 00:27:12.440
like do, sort of, A/B
testing between the users.

00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:15.680
And that's the user
experience, which

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:17.910
is available right
now, mostly on App

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:19.302
Engine and similar platforms.

00:27:19.302 --> 00:27:21.260
Because when you're on
Kubernetes, for example,

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:26.600
you have to scale blue green
in a more involved fashion.

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:32.250
So if the runtimes fit you,
I recommend you try it out.

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:35.470
OK, so I will hand
over to Freek now

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:37.250
to present the customer
example of Tentoo.

00:27:40.238 --> 00:27:43.724
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:54.067 --> 00:27:55.150
FREEK VAN DE GRIENDT: Mic.

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:55.858
Now it's working.

00:27:55.858 --> 00:27:57.340
Thank you.

00:27:57.340 --> 00:27:58.670
So I'm Freek van de Griendt.

00:27:58.670 --> 00:27:59.920
I'm a director at g-company.

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:02.350
We are a premiere
Google Cloud partner.

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:05.180
And we give trainings on all of
this fancy technology for all

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:07.030
of you developers
and DevOps out there.

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:08.620
We actually have
a booth here where

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:10.245
you can get a discount
on our training,

00:28:10.245 --> 00:28:11.500
so please check it out.

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:13.540
It's in the main hall.

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:16.000
But I'm not here today to
pitch you about our training.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:20.000
I'm here to tell you about a
customer example at Tentoo.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:24.820
So you've seen the many
examples of really, really

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:26.150
cool technology.

00:28:26.150 --> 00:28:28.120
And actually, what I
wanted to show you today

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:32.410
is what you can do with it in
a very, very simple case, which

00:28:32.410 --> 00:28:33.120
is Tentoo.

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:36.640
Tentoo is a payrolling company,
active both in the Netherlands

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:40.660
and in Belgium, and they have a
focus on the artistic and event

00:28:40.660 --> 00:28:41.590
sector.

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:44.680
Now just to tell you
something about what they do,

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:47.770
is they manage the part time
employment support people.

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:50.230
So working in the
artistic sector

00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:53.427
and doing events
like students guiding

00:28:53.427 --> 00:28:55.510
the people around here as
typically the activities

00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:57.310
that Tentoo does.

00:28:57.310 --> 00:29:01.480
Just for your information, they
manage the part time employment

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:05.110
of about 5,000 people on an
annual basis just in Belgium.

00:29:05.110 --> 00:29:07.750
And they do this with a
team of 15 people only.

00:29:07.750 --> 00:29:09.460
So that's full operations.

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:11.612
So they are one of the
first payrolling companies

00:29:11.612 --> 00:29:13.070
that have been
doing this for years

00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:16.030
to rely heavily on their
digital platform, which

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:18.610
they call My Tentoo.

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:22.620
So we've engaged with them
since the end of 2015.

00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:26.000
And when we first met them,
they had a couple of challenges.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:28.030
So first of all,
their infrastructure

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:30.540
was completely outdated.

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:34.540
It was a mixture
of six applications

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:36.970
that were all patched
together with old fashioned

00:29:36.970 --> 00:29:38.200
integrations.

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:41.260
And worst of all,
these applications

00:29:41.260 --> 00:29:43.960
were actually inherited from
the Dutch mother company.

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:46.620
So it was very, very
difficult for Tentoo

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:48.895
to follow with
Belgian legislation.

00:29:48.895 --> 00:29:51.520
If you do payrolling in Belgium,
if you know anything about it,

00:29:51.520 --> 00:29:53.490
it's quite complicated,
much more complicated

00:29:53.490 --> 00:29:54.490
than in the Netherlands.

00:29:54.490 --> 00:29:57.610
So it's a big
challenge, let alone

00:29:57.610 --> 00:29:59.950
that they could innovate
towards their customers.

00:29:59.950 --> 00:30:03.940
So their customers
go in this platform

00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:06.350
and create contracts
on a daily basis.

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:08.797
So the more modern,
the more usable

00:30:08.797 --> 00:30:11.130
the platform is, the bigger
their competitive advantage,

00:30:11.130 --> 00:30:15.140
which they want to increase
over time, obviously.

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:17.230
So Tentoo is a--

00:30:17.230 --> 00:30:20.200
I just mentioned it's 15
people, so it's a small company.

00:30:20.200 --> 00:30:21.070
It's an SMB.

00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:25.210
They don't have any dedicated
IT or network administrators

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:26.080
in-house.

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:28.640
So sometimes they
just were just facing

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:31.480
with the fact that their
internet connectivity went

00:30:31.480 --> 00:30:35.890
down, and so customers were
not able to create contracts,

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:38.950
just for a full day,
which obviously, for them,

00:30:38.950 --> 00:30:40.510
was a legal
nightmare, because you

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:43.570
have to comply to certain
DIMONA standards, et cetera.

00:30:43.570 --> 00:30:45.650
It's all very complicated.

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:46.510
It's bad.

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:50.350
So basically we went
and go and advise them

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:53.560
on how we could solve
this challenge using

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:54.490
Google technology.

00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:57.770
And that's what I want
to focus on today.

00:30:57.770 --> 00:31:01.580
So we have built for
them a web application.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:03.910
So it's a modern
web application that

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:06.460
basically works on any device.

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:08.620
We've used the App
Engine for that

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:11.770
because of many of the reasons
that Dmitriy just highlighted.

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:13.630
It's just we didn't
want to have anything

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:16.090
to do with moving servers
or maintaining them,

00:31:16.090 --> 00:31:18.130
maintaining versions, whatever.

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:20.630
We just wanted to start coding.

00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:23.620
So it's backend/frontend
split, so the backend

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:28.480
is in PHP, which was a
requirement by the Dutch mother

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:30.760
company because they wanted
to have some audibility,

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:33.280
and they have some PHP
in-house knowledge there,

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:34.540
which, for us, was fine.

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:36.230
App Engine was able to run that.

00:31:36.230 --> 00:31:40.450
And in the frontend,
we're using Angular, also

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:41.860
Google sponsored technology.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:45.070
And we chose that, mainly
because of the wide developer

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:47.727
audience there is for Angular.

00:31:47.727 --> 00:31:49.810
If you have really hardcore
JavaScript developers,

00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:52.660
you can discuss whether it's
the right framework to use.

00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:55.780
It was a very, very
usable one for us.

00:31:55.780 --> 00:31:58.150
Scalability is super
interesting as well.

00:31:58.150 --> 00:32:00.490
The loads on Tentoo's
web application

00:32:00.490 --> 00:32:04.720
is pretty much 24/7, a little
bit lower in the weekend.

00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:08.350
But it's pretty
much constant 24/7.

00:32:08.350 --> 00:32:11.362
And it's really just a handful
of high volume customers

00:32:11.362 --> 00:32:12.820
that are using a
functionality they

00:32:12.820 --> 00:32:15.040
call Timesheets, where
they want to create

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:17.920
1,000 contracts in one go.

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:22.110
And for that, obviously, when
such a timesheet is created,

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:25.210
App Engine automatically scales
up, creates new instances,

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:27.850
and when the timesheet is
fully processed and checked

00:32:27.850 --> 00:32:30.310
with all of the minimum
wages and all the payroll

00:32:30.310 --> 00:32:32.140
logic that's behind
it, App Engine

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:33.550
automatically scales down.

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:37.420
It's pretty powerful for Tentoo.

00:32:37.420 --> 00:32:41.230
Secondly, we use Cloud SQL
as a reliable database.

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:43.120
We had choices of
databases, obviously.

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:45.430
We chose a relational
one, basically,

00:32:45.430 --> 00:32:48.280
because we want to do a
lot of complex querying,

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:50.950
and we have pretty
high volume of records,

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:54.450
but not that much that you
would be looking into BigQuery

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:58.690
or Cloud Spanner that wouldn't
make much sense for Tentoo.

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:02.710
All of the contracts and,
basically, all the files

00:33:02.710 --> 00:33:06.130
that are exchanged with
customers and the people that

00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:08.320
are employed-- so that's
employment contracts,

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:12.700
payslips, invoices-- they're
all stored on Cloud Storage.

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:16.900
So we've built this
application in about a year.

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:19.180
We migrated five years
of data, and now we've

00:33:19.180 --> 00:33:21.040
been live for about a month.

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:25.270
And there's half a million
files on Cloud Storage,

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:27.580
obviously without any
degrade of performance

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:31.720
and requiring no
service base on-prem.

00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:34.840
There are still some highly
specialized applications

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:36.124
in the on premise network.

00:33:36.124 --> 00:33:37.540
One of them is the
payroll engine.

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:40.360
The other is the
accounting software.

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:44.020
And we've built interfaces
with those applications.

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:46.210
It's basically standard
web services as well as

00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:48.040
FTP file exchange.

00:33:48.040 --> 00:33:50.950
Still pretty old fashioned.

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:56.950
In this project, we've also
moved a really horrible legacy

00:33:56.950 --> 00:33:59.620
application to the cloud,
which was basically

00:33:59.620 --> 00:34:03.400
trying to do a mix
between customer support

00:34:03.400 --> 00:34:05.320
as well as CRM,
which we've covered

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:07.480
with freshdesk and freshsales.

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:09.711
These are both G Suite
recommended applications

00:34:09.711 --> 00:34:10.210
as well.

00:34:10.210 --> 00:34:12.270
So it's a really nice match.

00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:15.190
And we integrate
with them two way--

00:34:15.190 --> 00:34:18.190
we built the two way
sync using standard APIs

00:34:18.190 --> 00:34:20.590
from those products,
as well as APIs

00:34:20.590 --> 00:34:23.230
that we built in App
Engine and choose

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:27.520
to expose for those
specific integrations.

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:31.520
In terms of reporting,
we tend to want

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:34.250
it to have some kind of
BI functionality as well.

00:34:34.250 --> 00:34:37.090
So we have evaluated
several platforms.

00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:39.190
We looked into
Tableau, which was

00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:43.600
quite heavy for the solution
that Tentoo was looking at.

00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:46.029
Then on the other hand,
Cloud Data Studio,

00:34:46.029 --> 00:34:50.650
also integrated with G Suite,
was a little bit too limited.

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:52.730
So we chose to go for Periscope.

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:55.630
Periscope is a SAS
platform that you just

00:34:55.630 --> 00:34:57.010
connect to your database.

00:34:57.010 --> 00:34:58.960
You, I think, connect
to the Cloud SQL,

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:00.610
and then you will
be creating nice

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:02.830
looking dashboards that
you can send to people

00:35:02.830 --> 00:35:04.230
in a matter of hours, really.

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:11.150
So that's about the architecture
that we're on right now.

00:35:11.150 --> 00:35:16.120
So again, we rebuilt the
entire application from scratch

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:18.190
in less than a year,
and we've been live

00:35:18.190 --> 00:35:20.500
now for about a month.

00:35:20.500 --> 00:35:22.890
We're still in Hypercare,
so still facing some post

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:24.580
go live issues as you always do.

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:28.090
But we use the versioning of
App Engine, actually, for that.

00:35:28.090 --> 00:35:31.720
So we push a new version to
production at least once a day.

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:33.580
And then while we
deploy this new version,

00:35:33.580 --> 00:35:37.450
we split the traffic between
the internal people of Tentoo,

00:35:37.450 --> 00:35:39.580
so that they can run a new
version while customers

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:42.940
are still on the older
production version, which

00:35:42.940 --> 00:35:45.010
is like a form of A/B testing.

00:35:45.010 --> 00:35:47.270
And as soon as we know that
there are no bugs anymore,

00:35:47.270 --> 00:35:49.228
that's actually when we
move all of the traffic

00:35:49.228 --> 00:35:50.450
to the latest version.

00:35:50.450 --> 00:35:53.320
So that works pretty nicely,
and App Engine as well,

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:56.590
without us being
experts on the subject.

00:35:56.590 --> 00:35:59.590
So what's next for Tentoo?

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:02.380
Obviously, we still have
those on-prem applications

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:06.850
that's a payroll engine,
which is pretty sophisticated.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:08.860
It's an application
running on .NET.

00:36:08.860 --> 00:36:11.410
So we're very happy to hear
that, just recently, we're

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:16.300
also able to move that
to Compute Engine.

00:36:16.300 --> 00:36:21.100
Secondly, Tentoo is still
using tape, old tape storage,

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:24.610
for their full historical
archive of invoices

00:36:24.610 --> 00:36:28.040
and contracts that they want
to move to the Cloud as well.

00:36:28.040 --> 00:36:30.280
And lastly, there is
actually a parallel session

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.050
going on about Apigee.

00:36:32.050 --> 00:36:36.280
Apigee is a platform for
managing and developing APIs.

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:40.660
And given that the new
might tend to run APIs,

00:36:40.660 --> 00:36:43.910
we actually want to
consider using Apigee to see

00:36:43.910 --> 00:36:45.340
to which degree they are used.

00:36:45.340 --> 00:36:47.860
Because some of these high
volume customers from Tentoo

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:49.460
that I mentioned
are actually-- they

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:50.835
have their own IT
infrastructure,

00:36:50.835 --> 00:36:53.830
and they actually want to create
the contracts through API.

00:36:53.830 --> 00:36:57.190
So we would be using Apigee to
monitor the traffic, as well

00:36:57.190 --> 00:37:00.830
as [INAUDIBLE] and the
errors that might occur.

00:37:00.830 --> 00:37:02.500
So that was the
most important thing

00:37:02.500 --> 00:37:05.030
I wanted to share on how we
use this wonderful technology.

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:07.289
And I'll pass it
back to Dmitriy.

00:37:07.289 --> 00:37:08.455
DMITRIY NOVAKOVSKIY: Thanks.

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:17.680
OK, so now, as a matter
of a bit of dessert,

00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:21.050
we have one last service
called Cloud Functions.

00:37:21.050 --> 00:37:25.000
And Cloud Functions allows
us to deploy the code which

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:29.770
will be executed based on the
event with no infrastructure

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:33.310
involved and, actually, no
even the notion of server

00:37:33.310 --> 00:37:35.150
or application involved.

00:37:35.150 --> 00:37:36.064
So what this means.

00:37:36.064 --> 00:37:37.480
We get the serverless
environments

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:40.630
to build and connect
Cloud Services.

00:37:40.630 --> 00:37:45.130
Fully managed execution, so all
the functions that are written,

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:47.150
they execute in
no gs environment.

00:37:47.150 --> 00:37:48.920
And you pay per execution time.

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:52.720
So you don't even pay for,
like, constant uptime.

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:54.026
What event driven means.

00:37:54.026 --> 00:37:55.900
Event driven means that
you write a function.

00:37:55.900 --> 00:38:00.250
Let's imagine that this
is a function that at,

00:38:00.250 --> 00:38:04.330
like, the meme subscription to
a picture that was uploaded.

00:38:04.330 --> 00:38:08.650
And this function executes
only when some event

00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:10.270
within the Cloud happens.

00:38:10.270 --> 00:38:12.340
One example is Cloud
pops up message.

00:38:12.340 --> 00:38:14.120
So there is a message
in a message queue.

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:15.960
The function triggers
whatever-- these

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:18.282
are symbols what's in the
message-- puts it somewhere.

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:22.990
Cloud Storage event.

00:38:22.990 --> 00:38:24.520
The new picture
is being uploaded,

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:28.235
the function runs, adds the
funny signature to the picture.

00:38:28.235 --> 00:38:31.570
It should be request
on API endpoints,

00:38:31.570 --> 00:38:33.620
or web hook,
something like this.

00:38:33.620 --> 00:38:34.690
So again, function runs.

00:38:34.690 --> 00:38:36.220
You pay for a couple of seconds.

00:38:36.220 --> 00:38:37.480
It goes down.

00:38:37.480 --> 00:38:39.520
And also a bunch of
events from Firebase.

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:41.500
So for those of you
familiar with Firebase,

00:38:41.500 --> 00:38:44.170
that's the mobile development
platform, which is also now

00:38:44.170 --> 00:38:46.600
part of Google Cloud,
which allows us also

00:38:46.600 --> 00:38:48.550
to do the triggers
based on analytics,

00:38:48.550 --> 00:38:50.560
like certain threshold
of customer behavior

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:53.890
was met with
authentication, customer

00:38:53.890 --> 00:38:56.290
has just logged in into
application, and so on,

00:38:56.290 --> 00:38:57.760
so forth.

00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:01.090
Also around all these products
from different Cloud platforms

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:03.280
which run event
driven functions,

00:39:03.280 --> 00:39:05.650
there is now a
movement of frameworks,

00:39:05.650 --> 00:39:07.930
of serverless
frameworks, which allow

00:39:07.930 --> 00:39:13.870
developers like you to build
fully functional applications

00:39:13.870 --> 00:39:17.980
relying fully on Cloud
Functions or similar products

00:39:17.980 --> 00:39:19.220
from other platforms.

00:39:19.220 --> 00:39:20.290
So no servers at all.

00:39:20.290 --> 00:39:22.512
Everything is compute
completely even driven.

00:39:22.512 --> 00:39:24.220
I haven't yet actually
seen this in life,

00:39:24.220 --> 00:39:26.060
but I would really want to see.

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:28.900
And Cloud Functions
has been recently added

00:39:28.900 --> 00:39:31.480
as a support platform
for serverless.com.

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:35.480
That's one of the frameworks
which allow you to do that.

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:37.660
So when is it when
Cloud Functions

00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:38.860
would be considered a fit.

00:39:38.860 --> 00:39:41.080
So what code would
you put there.

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:44.170
So first of all, something that
is serverless by its nature--

00:39:44.170 --> 00:39:46.720
so after you read a bunch of
articles about serverless,

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:49.624
you will realize that
some specific function,

00:39:49.624 --> 00:39:51.790
or some bunch of functions,
within your application,

00:39:51.790 --> 00:39:53.950
are within the solution
that you are building.

00:39:53.950 --> 00:39:56.125
They don't actually
need to constantly run

00:39:56.125 --> 00:39:58.390
in backend, middle,
or whatever, they just

00:39:58.390 --> 00:40:01.270
need to react to certain
events, like a file was uploaded

00:40:01.270 --> 00:40:04.430
somewhere or a message was
received in the message queue.

00:40:04.430 --> 00:40:06.730
So same as with Docker.

00:40:06.730 --> 00:40:09.190
At some point within your
team, or maybe it was yourself,

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:10.731
you will see that
the people who will

00:40:10.731 --> 00:40:12.984
just say, why would we
deploy it on App Engine?

00:40:12.984 --> 00:40:15.400
Lets put a function on it, and
let's put the trigger on it

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:20.220
to run whenever the new file
is arriving, like with Eto,

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:20.920
obviously.

00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:24.820
So if you need to transform
a bunch of text received

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:27.580
from somewhere, or if you need
to transform even a bigger data

00:40:27.580 --> 00:40:30.619
structure, if it's not fit
for Hadoop or Dataflow,

00:40:30.619 --> 00:40:32.410
you might as well do
it with Cloud Function

00:40:32.410 --> 00:40:34.430
with minimal spending.

00:40:34.430 --> 00:40:36.400
Lightweight API
endpoints and web hooks.

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:39.220
As I mentioned earlier, so Cloud
Function-- when you deploy it,

00:40:39.220 --> 00:40:43.270
it can be triggered by, for
example, some requests which

00:40:43.270 --> 00:40:49.210
it received on a public
a-point and also as sort

00:40:49.210 --> 00:40:51.740
of a glue between
the Cloud Services.

00:40:51.740 --> 00:40:56.200
So again, instead of writing
some backend engine which

00:40:56.200 --> 00:40:58.570
will be sitting there and
listening to the message queue

00:40:58.570 --> 00:41:01.150
and then trying to orchestrate
all the other services,

00:41:01.150 --> 00:41:03.790
Cloud Functions can
serve this purpose.

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:06.736
So if you need to react to
certain events happening

00:41:06.736 --> 00:41:08.860
within your cloud, and if
you want your application

00:41:08.860 --> 00:41:10.840
to react and other
Cloud Services to react,

00:41:10.840 --> 00:41:13.540
Cloud Functions
is the way it go.

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:15.050
The constraints.

00:41:15.050 --> 00:41:17.290
First of all, right
now only single runtime

00:41:17.290 --> 00:41:18.700
is available for
Cloud Functions,

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:20.324
so you have to write
them on JavaScript

00:41:20.324 --> 00:41:23.450
and execute in managed
no gs environment.

00:41:23.450 --> 00:41:25.100
Function a little granularity.

00:41:25.100 --> 00:41:28.040
So usually each function
contains only one function.

00:41:28.040 --> 00:41:30.770
So they are single
purpose, pretty much,

00:41:30.770 --> 00:41:33.430
and must interact via events.

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:36.310
So you cannot call one function
directly from another function.

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:38.268
You have to put the
message into message queue,

00:41:38.268 --> 00:41:42.360
and then the other
function will react to it.

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:46.090
OK, so we have 3 and
1/2 minutes left.

00:41:46.090 --> 00:41:48.732
So where should I run my code.

00:41:48.732 --> 00:41:50.440
You have to choose
the abstraction layer.

00:41:50.440 --> 00:41:52.480
You have to consider your
technical requirements.

00:41:52.480 --> 00:41:53.771
You have to consider your team.

00:41:53.771 --> 00:41:56.860
So is your team ready to
adopt Kubernetes and work

00:41:56.860 --> 00:41:58.150
in micro services?

00:41:58.150 --> 00:42:02.050
Is your team ready to
conform to App Engine runtime

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:02.860
requirements?

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:06.080
And what is the comfortable
abstraction layer for you?

00:42:06.080 --> 00:42:08.950
Do you want to
manage VMs or not?

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:11.760
Technical requirements
move you down the stack.

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:16.030
So if you need access
to GBUs, for example,

00:42:16.030 --> 00:42:18.850
for fast processing, or if you
need a specific Linux kernel,

00:42:18.850 --> 00:42:21.100
you will have to run
with Compute Engine.

00:42:21.100 --> 00:42:24.460
If you want to do Hybrid Cloud
or if you need certain network

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:27.310
protocols, you can stick
with Container Engine

00:42:27.310 --> 00:42:29.980
and keep Compute
Engine as abstraction.

00:42:29.980 --> 00:42:31.480
If you have the
constraints in terms

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:33.430
of what programming
languages you want to use

00:42:33.430 --> 00:42:35.130
and what runtimes
you want to use,

00:42:35.130 --> 00:42:38.410
that's the App Engine moment.

00:42:38.410 --> 00:42:39.630
Also with team organizations.

00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:43.210
So Functions and App
Engine are mostly--

00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:46.260
they alert to the teams
that are dev focused.

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:48.220
Container Engine
are the teams which

00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:50.440
very well interact with
each other, like dev

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:52.007
teams, ops teams.

00:42:52.007 --> 00:42:54.340
And Compute Engine can be
used pretty much for anything.

00:42:54.340 --> 00:42:55.730
We've been running
VMs for years.

00:42:55.730 --> 00:42:57.550
Come on.

00:42:57.550 --> 00:43:01.600
So if at this point, at
the end of the conference,

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:03.550
at the end of the
talk, you find yourself

00:43:03.550 --> 00:43:07.662
at sort of a moment of
making a risky decision,

00:43:07.662 --> 00:43:10.120
and you're saying, like, OK,
if I move this Compute Engine,

00:43:10.120 --> 00:43:12.411
will I be able to live with
it for the next five years,

00:43:12.411 --> 00:43:16.300
and so on, we would like to
tell you, don't be worried.

00:43:16.300 --> 00:43:19.750
Because most of the customers,
and most of the large scale

00:43:19.750 --> 00:43:22.210
deployments, are actually
using a combination.

00:43:22.210 --> 00:43:24.310
So nobody, never,
will restrict you

00:43:24.310 --> 00:43:26.890
from running some stuff
on Cloud Functions.

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:29.020
Even more, most
likely, Cloud Functions

00:43:29.020 --> 00:43:32.800
will be like an additional
capability for you towards--

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:35.320
next to Kubernetes cluster
or to Compute Engine.

00:43:35.320 --> 00:43:37.090
High performance
stuff, or legacy stuff,

00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:40.210
or customized stuff can
run on Compute Engine.

00:43:40.210 --> 00:43:43.360
Micro Services can run
on Container Engine.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:46.330
So find the right fit
for you, based on this,

00:43:46.330 --> 00:43:48.690
based on what you read, based
on what we can talk about

00:43:48.690 --> 00:43:49.900
after the conference.

00:43:49.900 --> 00:43:53.380
And always be sure that you
can migrate between them.

00:43:53.380 --> 00:43:55.030
Because again, within
the Google Cloud,

00:43:55.030 --> 00:43:57.350
you will use Cloud
Storage for the storage,

00:43:57.350 --> 00:44:00.190
Cloud SQL as a database,
or Spanner as a database.

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:02.020
And all of these
services are very nicely

00:44:02.020 --> 00:44:04.450
integrated with each other,
so is the case through APIs,

00:44:04.450 --> 00:44:05.140
and so on.

00:44:05.140 --> 00:44:07.960
So if, at some point, you want
to move your PHP code off App

00:44:07.960 --> 00:44:11.500
Engine into Kubernetes, we have
tons of customer experience,

00:44:11.500 --> 00:44:16.090
of customer examples who were
actually able to do that.

00:44:16.090 --> 00:44:19.300
So too long, didn't read.

00:44:19.300 --> 00:44:21.050
Just a quick recap
of everything.

00:44:21.050 --> 00:44:25.000
Cloud Functions, event
driven, single purpose.

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:29.140
App Engine, mostly web
facing code and mobile or web

00:44:29.140 --> 00:44:32.920
backends, restricted in runtime,
but very easy to manage.

00:44:32.920 --> 00:44:35.310
Just code, no operations at all.

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:37.540
Container Engine, modern,
containerized workloads

00:44:37.540 --> 00:44:40.210
and Micro Services
architectures, most likely

00:44:40.210 --> 00:44:42.700
if you need it, or you are
already talking about it,

00:44:42.700 --> 00:44:45.010
or some other people
are talking about it.

00:44:45.010 --> 00:44:47.780
Compute Engine, pretty
much everything else.

00:44:47.780 --> 00:44:51.200
Lift and shift control
over operating system,

00:44:51.200 --> 00:44:54.760
just really like to manage Linux
operating systems [INAUDIBLE]..

00:44:54.760 --> 00:44:58.330
So everything that you cannot
see the fit in the other layers

00:44:58.330 --> 00:44:59.880
of the stack, we can fit it.

00:45:03.767 --> 00:45:04.600
That's it for today.

00:45:04.600 --> 00:45:05.500
Thank you.

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:08.550
[APPLAUSE]

